st card that wins — ideally preserving higher ones.\n\nBut note: there\'s no K or 10 in my hand, and the unknown counts show K:3, Q:2, J:2 — meaning many face cards remain. So someone could easily overtake a lower high card.\n\nHowever, **this is not a free play** — I must respond to ♠9. Passing is allowed per rules even if I can beat it, but doing so gives the landlord a free trick, which helps them empty their hand faster — bad for peasants.\n\nTherefore, I should play the weakest card that still beats ♠9, to preserve stronger cards (like ♦2 and ♠A) for future critical moments.\n\nAmong the valid beating singles:\n- ♥10 > 9 → valid\n- ♥J > 10 → better\n- ♦Q > J → stronger\n- ♠A → very strong\n- ♦2 → strongest non-joker\n\nBest choice: **♥10** — it barely wins, minimizes escalation, and saves higher cards for later when control is more crucial.\n\nReason: Playing ♥10 beats the current trick with minimal commitment, conserving high cards like ♦2 and ♠A for later control opportunities. Since the landlord still has many cards and may attempt long sequences or bombs, keeping powerful singles is vital. Also, my teammate seems weak (passed earlier), so I need to maintain potential control without overextending.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥10"]}'}
